WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.075
The governing council
 decided the following:

00:00:10.243 --> 00:00:15.382
First - We will continue to
 conduct net asset purchases

00:00:15.815 --> 00:00:20.954
under the pandemic emergency
 purchase program with a total envelope

00:00:21.087 --> 00:00:27.627
of €1,800 billion, until at least
 the end of March 22

00:00:28.161 --> 00:00:31.798
and in any case
 until the governing council judges

00:00:32.332 --> 00:00:35.368
that the coronavirus crisis
 phase is over.

00:00:39.773 --> 00:00:41.875
Morning Joe,
 you have the European Central Bank

00:00:42.375 --> 00:00:44.911
doing what economists
 thought they were going to do.

00:00:45.145 --> 00:00:49.949
They added to their pandemic emergency
 purchase fund by €500 billion.

00:00:49.949 --> 00:00:54.187
By my count, that takes
 it up to 1.85 trillion total.

00:00:58.291 --> 00:00:59.526
It is an absolutely historic,

00:00:59.526 --> 00:01:01.761
both in terms of the
speed of FED purchases

00:01:02.062 --> 00:01:04.064
and of course, the magnitude.

00:01:04.064 --> 00:01:05.165
Here's a chart that shows

00:01:05.165 --> 00:01:08.902
what's happened since
 March, the last three weeks

00:01:08.902 --> 00:01:13.807
have seen this huge ramp up in a ways
 that you've never seen before.

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:15.742
And let's look at what's changed here

00:01:15.742 --> 00:01:19.112
since the FED last night,
 we got to $1.9 trillion in relief

00:01:19.112 --> 00:01:20.814
enacted by Congress,
 signed by the president.

00:01:20.814 --> 00:01:26.252
Senate Democrats have just released the
 text of their $3.5 trillion budget resolution.

00:01:26.252 --> 00:01:30.056
Can you characterize everything
 that the FED has done this past week

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:33.026
as essentially flooding the system
 with money?

00:01:33.226 --> 00:01:34.594
Yes, exactly.

00:01:34.594 --> 00:01:37.163
And there's no end to your ability
 to do that.

00:01:37.163 --> 00:01:39.566
There is no end to our ability
 to do that.

00:01:39.566 --> 00:01:41.634
Simply flooded the system with money.

00:01:43.069 --> 00:01:44.304
Yes, we did.

00:01:44.304 --> 00:01:46.139
That's another way to think about it.

00:01:46.139 --> 00:01:46.773
We did.

00:01:46.773 --> 00:01:49.242
Where does it come from,
 do you just print it?

00:01:49.642 --> 00:01:51.077
We print it digitally.

00:01:51.077 --> 00:01:56.716
So we, you know, we as a central bank,
 we have the ability to create money digitally.

00:01:56.716 --> 00:02:00.487
And we do that by buying
 treasury bills or bonds

00:02:01.287 --> 00:02:03.056
or other government
 guaranteed securities

00:02:03.056 --> 00:02:05.225
and that actually increases
 the money supply.

00:02:05.925 --> 00:02:11.698
We do believe that inflation numbers
 in 21, which we will see rising.

00:02:11.965 --> 00:02:14.267
I can't find any period in history

00:02:14.901 --> 00:02:18.171
where monetary and fiscal policy
 were this out of step

00:02:18.171 --> 00:02:20.273
with the economic circumstances,
 not one.

00:02:20.273 --> 00:02:24.878
In six ways last spring.

00:02:25.578 --> 00:02:29.015
We did more QE,
 more purchasing of treasuries

00:02:29.482 --> 00:02:34.754
than we did the entire time in a
 9 year period from 2009 to 2018.

00:02:34.787 --> 00:02:38.024
And if we ever get into inflationary
 psychology, like for instance,

00:02:38.258 --> 00:02:41.694
we did when I was in my twenties

00:02:41.995 --> 00:02:44.931
back in the seventies,
 if we ever get that again.

00:02:45.632 --> 00:02:50.436
And if you ever got retail
 actually nervous about inflation,

00:02:50.570 --> 00:02:54.841
then the one thing that leads
 inflation, which is commodity prices,

00:02:54.841 --> 00:02:56.876
or it's the easiest tautology
 there is,

00:02:56.876 --> 00:03:02.549
those things can literally scream double
 or triple with no problem whatsoever.

00:03:02.549 --> 00:03:07.453
And valuations for both interest
 rates and stocks are at,

00:03:08.087 --> 00:03:11.391
if you combine the two,
 they're so overvalued,

00:03:11.391 --> 00:03:13.493
they're at 100 year highs.

00:03:13.493 --> 00:03:14.394
I don't know what you mean.

00:03:14.394 --> 00:03:17.964
I am so afraid of democracy

00:03:18.598 --> 00:03:22.101
getting the idea that you can just
 print money to solve all problems.

00:03:23.303 --> 00:03:25.104
And eventually I know that will fail.

00:03:25.104 --> 00:03:31.511
The end, if you print too much,
 you end up in something like Venezuela.

00:03:31.911 --> 00:03:32.946
It's mathematics.

00:03:32.946 --> 00:03:36.950
The fiat currency is now
 the error term that solves

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:41.421
the growth in the numerator,
 which is your total global debt

00:03:41.721 --> 00:03:45.725
versus the denominator,
 which is total global GDP.

00:03:45.925 --> 00:03:49.896
And we have reached a point
 of no return where the numerator

00:03:49.896 --> 00:03:53.333
is going to outstrip the
 growth of the denominator

00:03:53.333 --> 00:03:58.605
under any plausible scenario,
 which means you need to print money

00:03:58.771 --> 00:04:01.941
to solve that debt.

00:04:13.186 --> 00:04:17.757
We've all heard of our economic cycles and
 how according to most modern economy books,

00:04:17.757 --> 00:04:20.927
it is normal to have a period
 of very quick growth and expansion,

00:04:21.294 --> 00:04:24.330
followed by a period of contraction
 and economical crisis,

00:04:25.031 --> 00:04:29.869
as described in 1946 by Arthur F. Burns,
 former counselor to the president

00:04:29.869 --> 00:04:33.506
of the United States, and Wesley C.
 Mitchell, the American economist.

00:04:34.173 --> 00:04:36.843
Business cycles are a type
 of fluctuation found

00:04:36.843 --> 00:04:39.579
in the aggregate economic activity
 of nations.

00:04:39.979 --> 00:04:43.416
A cycle consists of expansions
 occurring at about the same time

00:04:43.416 --> 00:04:47.320
in many economic activities, followed
 by similarly general recessions.

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:50.690
This sequence of changes is recurrent,
 but not periodic.

00:04:51.357 --> 00:04:54.627
History, though, shows us that
 before the 20th century,

00:04:54.894 --> 00:04:57.463
financial crisis arrived
 because of external events.

00:04:58.164 --> 00:04:59.699
The most popular being war.

00:05:15.348 --> 00:05:17.450
There had been only
 one financial crisis

00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:21.387
not attributable to external events,
 and this was the panic of 1825,

00:05:21.921 --> 00:05:25.091
where around 70 banks went bankrupt
 due to risky investments.

00:05:25.391 --> 00:05:27.660
And because of this man,
 Gregor McGregor,

00:05:27.827 --> 00:05:29.128
that had pulled big investments

00:05:29.128 --> 00:05:32.365
into colonizing the country,
 that didn't exist - Poyais.

00:05:35.668 --> 00:05:40.506
If we look at history, what turns out,
 when we find out is the fact that

00:05:41.774 --> 00:05:44.777
this cycle really started
 about 100 years ago,

00:05:45.511 --> 00:05:47.347
and there's an important factor
 to that.

00:05:47.347 --> 00:05:53.086
It's the fact that in the year
 in 1914, every big nation in the world

00:05:53.086 --> 00:05:56.255
just started leaving the gold
 standard.

00:05:56.589 --> 00:05:59.392
Now, the gold standard is the fact
 that all of the money

00:05:59.392 --> 00:06:03.763
that a central bank has, controls
 or produces

00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:08.768
is only based on the amount of gold
 that they hold and the price of gold.

00:06:09.635 --> 00:06:12.572
Therefore, the amount of money

00:06:12.572 --> 00:06:15.441
supply available
 is relative to the gold that is held.

00:06:15.808 --> 00:06:17.377
This was dropped.

00:06:17.377 --> 00:06:21.681
And this is what led to a lot of
 financing for the first World War

00:06:21.681 --> 00:06:23.649
and even following
 the second World War,

00:06:23.649 --> 00:06:27.854
because governments
 realize that they have this huge power

00:06:28.054 --> 00:06:32.425
that is, get rid of the gold standard
 and we can just print money

00:06:32.425 --> 00:06:33.659
as much as we want.

00:06:33.659 --> 00:06:37.997
And in fact, this happens many times
 in history and was often the reason

00:06:38.197 --> 00:06:43.536
why governments, countries
 or civilizations were simply dropping.

00:06:43.803 --> 00:06:45.571
The Roman Empire is a
 great example of that.

00:06:45.571 --> 00:06:48.641
From the moment we dropped
 this gold standard,

00:06:48.641 --> 00:06:52.178
so around the year of 1914,
 the UK the first country to do that.

00:06:52.612 --> 00:06:54.347
This is the moment
 where we started seeing

00:06:55.815 --> 00:07:01.020
these short term and long  term cycles,
 particularly the short term cycles.

00:07:01.454 --> 00:07:04.490
We are now over 100 years
 after leaving the gold standard,

00:07:04.624 --> 00:07:07.994
and it is a fairly accepted fact
 that our economy works in cycles,

00:07:08.294 --> 00:07:12.064
based on a period of inflation,
 followed by a period of deflation.

00:07:12.598 --> 00:07:14.734
The fact this only started
 100 years ago

00:07:14.734 --> 00:07:16.936
should tell you
 that our monetary system has flaws

00:07:17.336 --> 00:07:20.640
all while being the reason for the
 unmatched growth we had as a species

00:07:20.807 --> 00:07:23.209
in the 20th century.

00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:33.519
This inflation is due to our reliance
 on debt and credit.

00:07:33.886 --> 00:07:36.155
According to modern monetary
 theory,

00:07:36.155 --> 00:07:39.492
debt is the driver of economic growth,
 not productivity.

00:07:39.959 --> 00:07:42.195
Ray Dalio explains this
 well in his video -

00:07:42.195 --> 00:07:43.830
How The Economic Machine Works.

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:48.701
Over time we learn and that accumulated
 knowledge raises our living standards,

00:07:48.801 --> 00:07:50.837
we call this productivity growth.

00:07:52.071 --> 00:07:55.775
Those who  are inventive and hard
 working raise their productivity

00:07:55.775 --> 00:08:00.012
and their living standards faster than
 those who are complacent and lazy.

00:08:00.913 --> 00:08:03.749
But that isn't necessarily true
 over the short run.

00:08:03.983 --> 00:08:06.252
Productivity matters
 most in the long run,

00:08:06.419 --> 00:08:08.821
but credit matters
 most in the short run.

00:08:09.222 --> 00:08:12.425
This is because productivity growth
 doesn't fluctuate much,

00:08:12.558 --> 00:08:15.328
so it's not a big driver of economic
 swings.

00:08:15.862 --> 00:08:19.699
Debt is, because it allows us
 to consume more than we produce

00:08:19.699 --> 00:08:21.000
when we acquire it,

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:23.803
and it forces us to consume
 less than we produce

00:08:23.970 --> 00:08:25.771
when we have to pay it back.

00:08:25.771 --> 00:08:28.708
And as stated by Dylan
 LeClair in his great article -

00:08:28.708 --> 00:08:32.011
The Conclusion of the Long Term
 Debt Cycle and the Rise of Bitcoin.

00:08:32.445 --> 00:08:35.214
Although productivity
 is the most important aspect

00:08:35.248 --> 00:08:39.218
of any economic system
 over the long term, not productivity

00:08:39.252 --> 00:08:40.720
with the forces of debt

00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:43.756
are the main driving forces
 in volatile economic swings.

00:08:44.891 --> 00:08:48.528
Coming back to the cycles, Ray Dalio
 describes the long term and the short

00:08:48.528 --> 00:08:51.531
term debt cycle and how they relate
 to human productivity.

00:08:51.831 --> 00:08:54.300
Debt swings occur in two big cycles.

00:08:55.001 --> 00:09:00.473
One takes about five to eight years and
 the other takes about 75 to 100 years.

00:09:00.706 --> 00:09:03.676
While most people feel the swings,
 they typically don't

00:09:03.676 --> 00:09:06.712
see them as cycles
 because they see them too up close.

00:09:06.812 --> 00:09:09.048
Day by day, week by week.

00:09:09.348 --> 00:09:11.450
The short term debt
 cycle can be observed

00:09:11.450 --> 00:09:14.453
by looking at different metrics,
 including the debt to income

00:09:14.453 --> 00:09:17.223
ratios and interest rates
 set by the central bank.

00:09:17.823 --> 00:09:21.193
Yes, the central bank essentially sets
 the rules that allow our economy

00:09:21.193 --> 00:09:25.698
to expand into unreasonable debt and
 later decides when it can break down.

00:09:26.399 --> 00:09:28.501
This is the so-called
 boom and bust cycle.

00:09:28.734 --> 00:09:32.371
The most recent ones being the global
 financial crisis of 2008

00:09:32.705 --> 00:09:34.874
and the dot com bubble
 of the year 2000.

00:09:35.975 --> 00:09:39.579
The long term debt cycle is made
 of multiple short term cycles.

00:09:40.179 --> 00:09:43.883
While our economy goes up and down
 during each of these cycles,

00:09:44.183 --> 00:09:46.586
it does bring growth in the long run.

00:09:46.586 --> 00:09:50.056
And with each cycle, our economy
 continues accumulating debt

00:09:50.690 --> 00:09:54.493
indefinitely because we prefer
 borrowing than repaying debt.

00:09:55.328 --> 00:09:58.698
There reaches a moment when there is
 more debt to pay than income.

00:09:59.332 --> 00:10:02.368
Historically, this is when the long
 term debt cycle shifts,

00:10:02.868 --> 00:10:05.438
people stop spending and
 start repaying debt.

00:10:05.972 --> 00:10:08.374
And instead of growing, we go down.

00:10:08.374 --> 00:10:10.576
We see recessions,
 increase government support,

00:10:10.876 --> 00:10:13.813
devaluation of currencies,
 social unrest and so on.

00:10:14.647 --> 00:10:17.650
There comes a time when our economy
 has sufficiently de-leveraged

00:10:17.817 --> 00:10:19.785
and the economy starts growing again

00:10:19.785 --> 00:10:23.089
following the short term debt
 cycle again.

00:10:23.222 --> 00:10:24.924
During these de-leveraging events,

00:10:24.924 --> 00:10:27.360
three strategies are adopted
 by central banks.

00:10:27.994 --> 00:10:30.262
First - Lower the interest rates.

00:10:30.262 --> 00:10:35.501
Interest rates are set by central
 banks and they set the

00:10:35.501 --> 00:10:39.305
rules as to what is the
 cost of borrowing money.

00:10:39.305 --> 00:10:42.775
If they lower it,
 then it's cheaper to borrow money.

00:10:43.109 --> 00:10:46.846
Therefore, people will be
 more inclined to borrow this money

00:10:47.446 --> 00:10:50.316
This leads to the spiral of
 just wanting to borrow more

00:10:50.316 --> 00:10:53.252
and more and more
 because it's just easier to borrow.

00:10:53.486 --> 00:10:56.889
And right now, if you look
 at the numbers the central bank,

00:10:57.790 --> 00:11:00.760
central banks all over the world
 have been doing this for years now

00:11:01.060 --> 00:11:03.896
because we work on a standard
 that is mostly based on the US dollar.

00:11:03.963 --> 00:11:10.403
What matters is what the U.S. central bank does,
 and if they lower interest rates,

00:11:10.403 --> 00:11:13.072
then everyone else will also
 lower their interest rates.

00:11:13.205 --> 00:11:16.842
This increases the value of assets
 and makes it easier to get credits.

00:11:17.743 --> 00:11:19.979
This is the first strategy used.

00:11:19.979 --> 00:11:23.082
Today, these interest rates
 have already dropped drastically

00:11:23.082 --> 00:11:26.018
for the main economies
 and have turned negative in many.

00:11:26.852 --> 00:11:28.554
If interest rates drop to zero,

00:11:28.554 --> 00:11:31.857
then there is no logical
 financial incentive to lend money.

00:11:32.525 --> 00:11:35.161
It can continue
 for a while until it doesn't.

00:11:37.163 --> 00:11:40.933
Second - There's quantitative easing,
 also called money printing.

00:11:41.267 --> 00:11:44.236
What this guy was talking about,
 it allows the central

00:11:44.236 --> 00:11:47.073
bank to buy debt
 securities and financial assets.

00:11:47.473 --> 00:11:51.210
It places cash in the hands of
 investors but doesn't help citizens,

00:11:51.510 --> 00:11:54.213
asset prices skyrocket,
 usually creating inflation,

00:11:54.647 --> 00:11:57.917
which makes asset holders
 that tend to be the wealthy, richer

00:11:58.317 --> 00:12:01.787
and the poor, poorer
 as their savings lose value.

00:12:02.321 --> 00:12:05.558
This is the case today,
 with real estate skyrocketing globally

00:12:05.725 --> 00:12:08.027
and other raw materials
 skyrocketing too.

00:12:10.229 --> 00:12:12.498
Third - And last, is increased welfare spending

00:12:12.498 --> 00:12:14.800
or other instruments,
 such as stimulus payments.

00:12:15.634 --> 00:12:19.438
If there is any kind of crisis,
 well the people that bought their house,

00:12:19.438 --> 00:12:22.508
they're not going to try
 and do any more financial

00:12:24.176 --> 00:12:26.412
schemes
 and things that would allow them to

00:12:26.412 --> 00:12:27.947
to protect their investment

00:12:27.947 --> 00:12:30.416
because they just don't
 have the knowledge or the skills

00:12:30.583 --> 00:12:32.651
and even the instruments
 to be able to do that.

00:12:32.651 --> 00:12:34.386
So they're the ones that lose the most
 right

00:12:34.386 --> 00:12:36.589
because investment banks
 think they know what's coming,

00:12:36.589 --> 00:12:38.724
they know how to deal with it
 and they'll get out of it.

00:12:39.091 --> 00:12:44.430
But this creates basically a gap
 between the rich and the poor.

00:12:44.430 --> 00:12:47.900
And this is a lot due to money
 printing because this money

00:12:48.834 --> 00:12:52.004
gets distributed into the economy,

00:12:52.004 --> 00:12:55.107
but it doesn't get distributed
 into the hands of people.

00:12:55.407 --> 00:12:59.445
It gets paid to banks,
 it gets paid to investors,

00:13:00.012 --> 00:13:01.881
and it just gives them
 another business.

00:13:01.881 --> 00:13:04.817
And it gives them more cash
 to be able to take on more positions

00:13:05.017 --> 00:13:08.988
and themselves invest  in to many different
 assets, whether it's the stock exchange,

00:13:09.321 --> 00:13:10.990
it can be gold, anything.

00:13:10.990 --> 00:13:14.627
The poorer people don't have these
 options and they don't have this money

00:13:14.627 --> 00:13:16.195
directly attributed to them.

00:13:16.195 --> 00:13:17.563
So it means that like, while

00:13:17.563 --> 00:13:19.598
all of this is happening
 and people are getting rich,

00:13:19.632 --> 00:13:20.633
others are getting poorer

00:13:20.633 --> 00:13:24.236
because the savings that they have in
 the bank are losing value

00:13:24.236 --> 00:13:27.173
because of this money printing
 and because of that,

00:13:27.173 --> 00:13:31.177
what governments need to do is
 they need to help their citizens more.

00:13:31.177 --> 00:13:32.711
Because of course,
 no one wants the wealth gap.

00:13:32.711 --> 00:13:36.182
I mean, it's not because you're,
 you know, part of this elite,

00:13:36.182 --> 00:13:40.119
let's say, that is in a better
 position financially,

00:13:40.419 --> 00:13:44.356
that you want the poor people
 to be in a bad situation

00:13:44.356 --> 00:13:47.860
like everyone has to be elevated
 in society and these people need

00:13:47.860 --> 00:13:52.731
to be helped directly through
 financing, whatever form it takes.

00:13:53.032 --> 00:13:55.467
And in fact,
 if you look at the numbers, it's

00:13:55.801 --> 00:14:00.105
since the crisis of 1929, which was
 the first big financial crisis

00:14:00.406 --> 00:14:03.676
after getting off the gold standard
 that I was mentioning at the beginning

00:14:04.176 --> 00:14:06.979
that this welfare spending
 has increased so much.

00:14:07.780 --> 00:14:11.383
And now in France, for example,
 there's about 30% of GDP

00:14:11.650 --> 00:14:13.352
that goes to welfare spending.

00:14:13.352 --> 00:14:14.620
France is quite famous for that.

00:14:14.620 --> 00:14:16.155
It has one of the best
 medical systems,

00:14:16.155 --> 00:14:19.458
but it also goes with how you support
 people that are unemployed.

00:14:20.226 --> 00:14:25.831
Different stimulus payments help for
 home allowances and this kind of thing,

00:14:26.098 --> 00:14:29.235
that is, of course, good for people
 that need it, but is only necessary

00:14:29.501 --> 00:14:31.570
because of these actions
 that are taken beforehand.

00:14:32.638 --> 00:14:33.639
More recently,

00:14:33.639 --> 00:14:36.876
we know social spending has increased
 due to the COVID crisis,

00:14:37.243 --> 00:14:40.346
so we can only presume that the chart
 now looks more like this.

00:14:43.682 --> 00:14:47.386
We are seeing another measure
 increasing quickly - the monetary supply.

00:14:47.386 --> 00:14:51.123
Monetary supply is the total
 amount of one currency

00:14:51.490 --> 00:14:54.426
that is currently available
 in the economy.

00:14:54.426 --> 00:14:58.030
The more government creates new money,
 the more the supply increases.

00:14:58.831 --> 00:15:03.002
This money supply is directly correlated
 to the devaluation of our currencies.

00:15:03.769 --> 00:15:07.139
Many like to inverse these charts
 in order to show this devaluation.

00:15:07.673 --> 00:15:10.976
Because the more currency is produced,
 the less it is scarce.

00:15:11.377 --> 00:15:14.179
Therefore,
 the more it loses value.

00:15:14.179 --> 00:15:16.382
We have all heard stories
 from our elders

00:15:16.382 --> 00:15:18.884
saying, money had a different
 value back then

00:15:19.518 --> 00:15:21.987
and I've seen archive images
 illustrating this.

00:15:22.621 --> 00:15:27.459
Like this McDonald's menu from 1972
 that had a Big Mac for $0.65.

00:15:28.894 --> 00:15:32.698
The increase in money supply is the
 reason why this happens.

00:15:32.698 --> 00:15:36.702
In 2020 alone, the money supply
 has had a big jump.

00:15:36.702 --> 00:15:40.339
This is the money that was printed in
 order to finance the war against COVID.

00:15:40.973 --> 00:15:43.709
And in the U.S.
 since the beginning of 2020,

00:15:43.709 --> 00:15:49.648
we have seen an increase of over 30%
 in the amount of U.S. dollars in circulation.

00:15:49.782 --> 00:15:53.185
Although this isn't felt instantly
 in the economy, the long term

00:15:53.185 --> 00:15:57.856
effects will be felt by the population
 that have zero allocation in assets

00:15:57.856 --> 00:16:00.459
such as real estate, stocks and so on.

00:16:01.493 --> 00:16:04.129
The long term consequences
 of this are very white.

00:16:04.763 --> 00:16:06.899
To illustrate, take technology

00:16:07.499 --> 00:16:10.402
by definition, technology
 should drop in price

00:16:10.402 --> 00:16:13.405
because it becomes more efficient
 and easier to produce.

00:16:14.373 --> 00:16:17.843
Yet, due to inflation,
 prices are not going down,

00:16:18.210 --> 00:16:21.046
essentially making it harder
 to develop new technologies.

00:16:22.181 --> 00:16:24.016
Governments use many reasons,

00:16:24.016 --> 00:16:27.052
including climate change,
 as an excuse to print trillions.

00:16:27.653 --> 00:16:28.787
But down the road,

00:16:28.787 --> 00:16:30.889
this printing can lead
 to adverse results

00:16:30.956 --> 00:16:32.825
because of the effects
 this new monetary

00:16:32.825 --> 00:16:36.161
supply can have on the development
 of the right technologies

00:16:36.161 --> 00:16:39.798
that could help us transition to a
 more renewable energy consuming world.

00:16:40.466 --> 00:16:42.534
But the central banks
 will have a different message.

00:16:42.901 --> 00:16:44.870
This is in order to avoid the spread
 of panic

00:16:44.870 --> 00:16:47.239
concerning the financial markets
 and their currencies,

00:16:47.373 --> 00:16:50.075
which could lead people to rush
 to banks to withdraw their money.

00:16:50.609 --> 00:16:53.245
This obviously would be unsustainable
 for the economy.

00:16:53.712 --> 00:16:57.216
A nation in which faith in a currency
 is lost will see recessions

00:16:57.249 --> 00:16:59.618
 and will take decades to recover.

00:16:59.618 --> 00:17:02.821
Instead, central banks
 use the consumer price index,

00:17:02.921 --> 00:17:04.990
also called the CPI.

00:17:04.990 --> 00:17:08.494
The CPI is a flawed
 indicator, yet is the most

00:17:08.494 --> 00:17:12.631
commonly accepted indicator to measure
 inflation and its effects on prices.

00:17:13.932 --> 00:17:15.467
The CPI follows the price

00:17:15.467 --> 00:17:17.970
of a basket of products
 that are consumed by people.

00:17:18.470 --> 00:17:21.740
This, in essence, is the way an
 indicator like this one should work.

00:17:22.408 --> 00:17:24.777
But the CPI is flawed
 because of the way

00:17:24.777 --> 00:17:27.413
this basket of products is selected.

00:17:27.413 --> 00:17:29.982
It is selected
 based on what people choose to buy.

00:17:30.883 --> 00:17:33.685
So every year, new products will be
 added to this basket

00:17:33.685 --> 00:17:36.555
while others will be removed.

00:17:37.356 --> 00:17:40.192
But what they choose to buy depends
 on the price of the product.

00:17:40.592 --> 00:17:43.462
If inflation goes up,
 people will change their basket

00:17:43.462 --> 00:17:46.131
of products in order to accommodate
 for the price increase.

00:17:46.865 --> 00:17:49.435
This essentially
 makes it a new basket of products.

00:17:50.069 --> 00:17:53.305
The CPI will not track the price
 of the previous basket of products.

00:17:53.705 --> 00:17:57.076
It will track the price
 of the new basket of products after

00:17:57.076 --> 00:18:00.446
the consumer decision has been made
 in response to price increases.

00:18:01.180 --> 00:18:04.483
Saifedean Ammous illustrates this
 properly in the Fiat standard.

00:18:05.417 --> 00:18:10.489
Imagine you earn $10 a day and spend
 them all on eating a delicious rib-eye steak

00:18:10.489 --> 00:18:12.825
that gives you all the nutrients
 you need for the day.

00:18:13.725 --> 00:18:17.663
In this simple consumer
 basket of goods, the CPI is $10.

00:18:18.397 --> 00:18:21.366
Now imagine one day
 hyperinflation strikes the economy,

00:18:21.600 --> 00:18:24.369
and the price of your rib-eye
 increases to $100

00:18:24.670 --> 00:18:26.839
while your daily wage remains $10.

00:18:27.606 --> 00:18:29.575
What happens to the price
 of your basket of goods?

00:18:30.709 --> 00:18:35.280
It cannot rise tenfold because you
 cannot afford the $100 rib-eye

00:18:35.280 --> 00:18:37.616
Instead, you make do
 with the chemical shitstorm

00:18:37.616 --> 00:18:40.052
that is a soy burger for $10.

00:18:40.052 --> 00:18:43.122
The CPI magically showed zero
 inflation.

00:18:44.189 --> 00:18:46.892
Remember that governments
 will never show us the true

00:18:46.892 --> 00:18:49.294
inflation numbers,
 and they will not attribute it

00:18:49.294 --> 00:18:52.531
to the increase in our monetary supply
 because of their management.

00:18:53.265 --> 00:18:57.102
If people actually understood this,
 they would never be re-elected.

00:19:00.572 --> 00:19:03.809
We've talked about debt so much,
 it is time to look at these numbers, too.

00:19:04.309 --> 00:19:08.480
We can see the sharp  increase of the global
 debt even in just the most recent years.

00:19:09.047 --> 00:19:10.449
To add more context,

00:19:10.449 --> 00:19:13.452
here is what this debt represents
 as share of global GDP.

00:19:14.920 --> 00:19:17.389
356%.

00:19:17.389 --> 00:19:22.961
We have 3.5 times more debt
 than actual created value.

00:19:22.961 --> 00:19:26.532
Now, this debt bubble could be stopped
 or at least be slowed down

00:19:26.765 --> 00:19:29.101
if the central banks were to increase
 interest rates,

00:19:29.501 --> 00:19:31.370
making it more expensive to borrow.

00:19:31.370 --> 00:19:33.939
Giving a breather
 to the entire system.

00:19:33.939 --> 00:19:35.941
But today it's likely too late.

00:19:36.408 --> 00:19:40.012
The U.S. central bank (the FED)
 attempted this in 2018

00:19:40.379 --> 00:19:41.847
because they believe the economy
 looked

00:19:41.847 --> 00:19:45.517
to have recovered from the global
 financial crisis of 2007.

00:19:46.485 --> 00:19:49.721
Ten years have now passed since
 the depths of the financial crisis,

00:19:50.722 --> 00:19:54.026
a painful part of our history
 that cost many Americans their jobs,

00:19:54.359 --> 00:19:56.695
their homes
 and for some, their hopes and dreams.

00:19:57.763 --> 00:20:00.799
In addition to holding interest rates
 low to support the recovery,

00:20:01.233 --> 00:20:04.269
we have also taken many steps
 to make the financial system safer.

00:20:05.037 --> 00:20:07.639
I'm confident
 that the system today is stronger

00:20:07.806 --> 00:20:10.709
and in a far better position
 to support the financial needs

00:20:10.709 --> 00:20:14.179
of households and businesses
 through good times and bad.

00:20:14.713 --> 00:20:18.383
They decided to increase these
 interest rates, and because of that,

00:20:18.817 --> 00:20:24.656
the entire market dropped in the space
 of a couple of weeks, and NASDAQ

00:20:24.656 --> 00:20:29.328
and the S&amp;P 500 dropped over 20% in
 just a couple of weeks.

00:20:29.561 --> 00:20:34.866
Only because the markets were reacting to these
 actions that were done by the central bank.

00:20:34.866 --> 00:20:37.536
The DOW is moving back towards
 the lows of the day.

00:20:37.903 --> 00:20:41.440
All 30 DOW stocks are now in the red,

00:20:41.440 --> 00:20:45.744
and the DOW's gains for the
 year are gone, a distant memory.

00:20:45.777 --> 00:20:48.313
The S&amp;P 500 has fallen
 into correction.

00:20:48.580 --> 00:20:51.717
That's a drop of 10%
 or more from recent highs.

00:20:52.150 --> 00:20:55.787
All sectors and this is key,
 are in the red at this moment,

00:20:55.787 --> 00:20:58.423
and the NASDAQ is now
 at a seven month low.

00:20:58.991 --> 00:21:02.961
Look at the CNN Business
 Fear and Greed Index.

00:21:03.061 --> 00:21:04.830
I know you don't want to see it,
 we got to give it to you.

00:21:04.830 --> 00:21:08.400
It measures volatility,
 momentum and demand for safe havens.

00:21:08.734 --> 00:21:11.603
It's pointing to extreme fear.

00:21:11.637 --> 00:21:15.040
So as soon as it started dropping,
 the central bank

00:21:15.641 --> 00:21:18.577
came publicly and said, OK,
 we're going to stop this.

00:21:19.011 --> 00:21:23.181
We're going to go back to normal
 level of interest rates.

00:21:23.415 --> 00:21:28.253
And from that moment
 on, by the end, in 2019,

00:21:28.253 --> 00:21:30.522
they started again
 offering these interest rates

00:21:30.789 --> 00:21:35.560
and they lowered them
 from all the way 2.5% to 0.25%,

00:21:36.194 --> 00:21:40.232
according to a lot of finance books
 and what people study at university;

00:21:40.432 --> 00:21:43.302
the scenario we're in today
 with negative interest rates

00:21:43.535 --> 00:21:45.604
is impossible, right?

00:21:45.604 --> 00:21:46.605
So what happens then?

00:21:46.605 --> 00:21:48.774
It's hard to say, no one really knows.

00:21:49.107 --> 00:21:53.211
We'll have to find out because anyway,
 central banks have no other option.

00:21:53.211 --> 00:21:55.280
The only thing they
 can do is print more money.

00:21:58.617 --> 00:22:02.187
In reality, the only thing
 that the central banks can do is print

00:22:02.187 --> 00:22:06.058
more money and cover for all this debt
 that is never being paid back.

00:22:06.792 --> 00:22:09.594
They will work with governments
 to continue increasing taxes,

00:22:09.594 --> 00:22:12.164
welfare spending
 and de-valuating currency.

00:22:12.731 --> 00:22:15.400
This isn't to say that these people
 are ill intended.

00:22:15.767 --> 00:22:17.602
They use the tools
 that are available to them

00:22:17.602 --> 00:22:20.272
and have simply reached the point
 where their backs are against the wall

00:22:20.672 --> 00:22:24.576
and they're forced to
 abuse these tools.

00:22:29.881 --> 00:22:33.885
And they're looking for solutions to take
 the entire economy out of this situation.

00:22:34.386 --> 00:22:37.689
Although these solutions are not
 necessarily in the best interests

00:22:37.689 --> 00:22:40.726
of citizens and their
 personal freedom.

00:22:40.726 --> 00:22:44.129
It isn't without reason that the World
 Economic Forums initiative is called

00:22:44.129 --> 00:22:47.332
the Great Reset, the name that
 inspired this documentary.

00:23:02.214 --> 00:23:07.018
Part of their plan is the creation of
 central bank digital currencies - CBDCs.

00:23:07.652 --> 00:23:10.522
This would allow central banks
 to have a new monetary system

00:23:10.756 --> 00:23:12.991
that they can detach
 from the current one,

00:23:12.991 --> 00:23:16.328
allowing people to transition
 into this new debt free system

00:23:16.728 --> 00:23:20.265
and slowly de-leveraging and dropping
 the debt from the previous one

00:23:20.599 --> 00:23:22.834
without adding
 risk to their currencies.

00:23:23.602 --> 00:23:26.872
So central bank
 digital currency is coming alive.

00:23:27.739 --> 00:23:29.508
It's not going to happen today.

00:23:29.508 --> 00:23:32.544
I think they have a twelve month
 experimental period, but

00:23:32.544 --> 00:23:36.281
they want to go through
 before they actually launch for good.

00:23:36.381 --> 00:23:38.884
The status of it is we're working
 hard on it right now.

00:23:39.217 --> 00:23:40.952
But let me tell you what it is,
 really.

00:23:40.952 --> 00:23:45.323
We're going to address digital payments broadly.

00:23:45.323 --> 00:23:48.527
So that means stablecoins,
 it means it means crypto assets,

00:23:48.527 --> 00:23:49.995
it means a CBDC.

00:23:49.995 --> 00:23:52.798
That whole group of issues

00:23:52.798 --> 00:23:55.934
and payment mechanisms,
 which we think are

00:23:56.902 --> 00:23:58.937
really at a critical point.

00:23:58.937 --> 00:24:04.576
Would you say that the corona crisis
 has even revealed more

00:24:04.676 --> 00:24:09.981
the need to have a digital central
 bank currency or currencies?

00:24:10.649 --> 00:24:19.591
Well, yes, I think the corona crisis has
 accelerated very much technical change

00:24:19.991 --> 00:24:24.563
and use of digital innovations
 across the board.

00:24:24.563 --> 00:24:27.566
I mean, it's not only in
 financial transactions,

00:24:27.966 --> 00:24:32.270
but in e-commerce
 and the show business.

00:24:32.504 --> 00:24:37.275
I mean, there are so many examples
 that, you know, but, it is a fact.

00:24:37.576 --> 00:24:40.512
Pretty much all central banks
 are thinking about this.

00:24:41.379 --> 00:24:45.050
In the last few weeks of 2020,
 the People's Bank of China

00:24:45.050 --> 00:24:48.887
rolled out a pilot program
 in the eastern Chinese city of Suzhou.

00:24:48.920 --> 00:24:52.190
They had to download an app and
 have it on the phone.

00:24:52.190 --> 00:24:56.428
In a macro way, you have a sense of
 how money flows through the economy.

00:24:56.461 --> 00:24:58.363
On a micro scale,
 and this is something

00:24:58.363 --> 00:25:00.899
that many in the West would
 probably not be comfortable with

00:25:00.899 --> 00:25:02.868
and many in China
 frankly, would not be comfortable with

00:25:02.868 --> 00:25:04.436
is that it would allow authorities

00:25:04.436 --> 00:25:08.240
to be able to track
 precisely how you or my neighbor

00:25:08.240 --> 00:25:10.342
or the person down the street
 is spending the money on.

00:25:10.342 --> 00:25:12.711
They're spending the money on buying
 things they shouldn't be buying.

00:25:12.711 --> 00:25:14.145
Whatever,
 however you define that.

00:25:14.145 --> 00:25:15.714
Are they gambling with their money?

00:25:15.714 --> 00:25:17.249
Are they doing this
 or that with the money?

00:25:17.249 --> 00:25:19.651
They say it's
 just to replace physical cash,

00:25:19.651 --> 00:25:22.787
but of course, this could just be
 the first step.

00:25:23.154 --> 00:25:25.257
Adoption will come for these CBDC's.

00:25:25.290 --> 00:25:27.192
In fact, it will be forced adoption.

00:25:28.527 --> 00:25:32.998
The government will start supporting citizens
 in need by only giving them stimulus payments

00:25:32.998 --> 00:25:35.634
through a wallet controlled
 directly by the central bank.

00:25:37.202 --> 00:25:39.204
The central bank
 will essentially be able

00:25:39.204 --> 00:25:42.173
to eliminate commercial banks
 that are currently the middleman

00:25:42.374 --> 00:25:45.243
between the central bank
 and the citizens.

00:25:45.243 --> 00:25:47.279
For governments,
 it will simplify many things.

00:25:47.646 --> 00:25:49.748
If they decide
 to change interest rates,

00:25:49.748 --> 00:25:52.851
they will be able to act on it
 directly rather than wait

00:25:52.851 --> 00:25:54.986
the several months needed
 for commercial banks

00:25:54.986 --> 00:25:56.922
to implement this in their systems.

00:25:56.922 --> 00:25:58.957
They will also be able to control
 directly

00:25:58.957 --> 00:26:02.661
the interest rates based on a person's
 profile or a business's profile,

00:26:02.827 --> 00:26:06.197
and will be able to set expiry dates
 on people's money,

00:26:06.531 --> 00:26:09.334
forcing them to spend
 and not allowing them to save.

00:26:10.268 --> 00:26:12.737
Raoul Pal describes this well.

00:26:12.737 --> 00:26:16.975
You see, central banks want to be able
 to give people money directly.

00:26:17.008 --> 00:26:18.410
Direct monetization.

00:26:19.644 --> 00:26:21.179
They can't do that right now.

00:26:21.179 --> 00:26:22.480
Right now,
they print money,

00:26:22.480 --> 00:26:24.215
goes into the banking system,
 the banks hoarde it

00:26:24.215 --> 00:26:26.318
because we're going through a credit
 crunch.

00:26:26.318 --> 00:26:29.654
It's also a way for them to kickstart
 universal basic income

00:26:30.021 --> 00:26:34.059
because the central bank
 can underpin

00:26:34.459 --> 00:26:37.295
the poorer parts of society
 by giving them money directly.

00:26:37.495 --> 00:26:40.265
It doesn't go on the government
 balance sheet.

00:26:40.732 --> 00:26:43.134
Now, central banks now believe
they're omnipotent,

00:26:43.501 --> 00:26:46.071
that they can continue
 to expand balance sheets forever.

00:26:46.371 --> 00:26:49.407
MMT seems to be the prevalent thought,

00:26:49.574 --> 00:26:51.343
and this is just an extension of this.

00:26:51.343 --> 00:26:54.746
This is kind of Keynesianism
 gone mad.

00:26:55.347 --> 00:26:58.917
Central banks can also change entirely

00:26:59.284 --> 00:27:05.590
the structure of  how money and
 monetary policy works and fiscal policy

00:27:05.991 --> 00:27:08.760
because they can give it
 to different people in different ways.

00:27:09.561 --> 00:27:12.797
So they can credit the restauranteur,
 but then penalize

00:27:12.797 --> 00:27:14.699
with negative interest rates.

00:27:14.699 --> 00:27:17.502
The Baby Boomer Saver
 because they want to release their

00:27:17.502 --> 00:27:18.837
money back into the economy.

00:27:19.804 --> 00:27:23.174
They can give students a positive
 interest rate to help them save.

00:27:23.575 --> 00:27:26.111
They can change everything.

00:27:26.111 --> 00:27:29.447
This is the rise of behavioral
 economics and incentive systems.

00:27:30.382 --> 00:27:33.318
So governments essentially using
 big data

00:27:33.318 --> 00:27:37.422
can find who they need to stimulate
 at any time and adjust accordingly.

00:27:37.422 --> 00:27:38.990
They can do it dynamically.

00:27:38.990 --> 00:27:43.595
This is a structural, massive shift
 to everything

00:27:43.595 --> 00:27:46.765
we understand about economics,
 particularly macro-economics.

00:27:47.832 --> 00:27:49.267
Nobody's prepared for this.

00:27:49.267 --> 00:27:51.169
None of us know what this means.

00:27:51.169 --> 00:27:54.572
It means and it will be sold on
 a lot of good things.

00:27:55.040 --> 00:27:57.475
And I think there's a lot
 of good things that come from this.

00:27:57.776 --> 00:28:01.079
I think it is an elegant solution
 to some of our problems.

00:28:01.446 --> 00:28:04.783
But elegant solutions in governments
 and central banks

00:28:05.150 --> 00:28:07.852
lead to unintended consequences.

00:28:08.753 --> 00:28:11.656
The issue is here,
 is to have this new system,

00:28:11.990 --> 00:28:13.324
you're going to give up your freedom.

00:28:14.526 --> 00:28:19.898
You are going to have every transaction
 you've ever done and ever will do recorded,

00:28:20.231 --> 00:28:23.935
There is no cash, there is no way
 of tipping the gardener

00:28:24.335 --> 00:28:25.804
unless it goes by cash.

00:28:26.571 --> 00:28:29.507
It means that they can tax you
 at every transaction level.

00:28:29.774 --> 00:28:30.575
Now that's great.

00:28:30.575 --> 00:28:32.010
We could get rid of the IRS

00:28:32.010 --> 00:28:36.448
and all of the tax collection agencies
 because it could be done directly.

00:28:37.115 --> 00:28:38.616
That's good.

00:28:38.616 --> 00:28:44.589
But again, you've lost your freedom to
 transact in anonymity that cash gives you.

00:28:55.266 --> 00:28:59.871
These are a central bank digital currencies,
 as I said, they're not,

00:28:59.871 --> 00:29:03.208
they're not an invention from
 governments and central banks.

00:29:03.441 --> 00:29:09.814
In fact, they're inspired by other digital
 currencies like bitcoin being the original one.

00:29:09.948 --> 00:29:12.383
And other altcoins
 that have been created after that.

00:29:13.418 --> 00:29:17.255
In reality,
 they're more similar to other altcoins

00:29:17.255 --> 00:29:21.059
such as Ethereum or others
 that simply allow the addition

00:29:21.059 --> 00:29:24.028
of programing that allows you
 to add functionality to them,

00:29:24.729 --> 00:29:28.933
whereas bitcoin is only there
 for these monetary transfers.

00:29:29.200 --> 00:29:33.004
Bitcoin is a payment network, right?

00:29:33.004 --> 00:29:37.208
Where this is... think of it
 as a log of transactions,

00:29:37.208 --> 00:29:38.810
a transaction that I can do to you.

00:29:38.810 --> 00:29:40.545
The Bitcoin network
 will take some information.

00:29:40.545 --> 00:29:42.714
So my address, your address,

00:29:42.947 --> 00:29:44.849
the amount of bitcoin
 that I'm sending to you.

00:29:44.849 --> 00:29:47.118
And with this information it's
 going to create a hash.

00:29:47.118 --> 00:29:49.087
It just goes through a simple
 hashing algorithm

00:29:49.087 --> 00:29:51.122
that makes the code
 out of this information.

00:29:51.122 --> 00:29:53.124
And this is added to this log.

00:29:53.158 --> 00:29:54.259
Whatever amounts that happen

00:29:54.259 --> 00:29:57.796
within these ten minutes,
 it basically is considered a block

00:29:58.096 --> 00:30:00.999
and there would be miners,
 so computers that are connected

00:30:00.999 --> 00:30:04.135
to the network
 to verify these transactions.

00:30:04.402 --> 00:30:06.104
So they're just going to be there
 really to confirm

00:30:06.104 --> 00:30:09.841
that I do have this bitcoin, and yes,
 I can send it to you.

00:30:10.074 --> 00:30:11.442
And then after that,

00:30:11.442 --> 00:30:14.279
I no longer have this bitcoin
 and you have the bitcoin.

00:30:14.412 --> 00:30:16.314
So very simple kind of work.

00:30:16.314 --> 00:30:19.717
But all of these computers
 are connected to the network

00:30:19.951 --> 00:30:24.756
and they're in competition
 fighting for who confirms the block

00:30:25.190 --> 00:30:29.027
because whoever confirms the block
 and verifies these transactions

00:30:29.360 --> 00:30:30.995
will be rewarded in bitcoin

00:30:31.529 --> 00:30:32.564
from two sources,

00:30:32.564 --> 00:30:34.699
There will be a transaction
 cost;

00:30:34.799 --> 00:30:38.136
simply, if I send your bitcoin,
 then I pay a certain fee,

00:30:38.403 --> 00:30:41.005
to the network, this fee will
 be redistributed to miners.

00:30:41.573 --> 00:30:46.611
And also, right now there is an emission of new bitcoin.

00:30:48.713 --> 00:30:51.249
Today it's at 6.25 bitcoins per block.

00:30:51.883 --> 00:30:55.153
And if I as a miner,
 I'm able to confirm this block,

00:30:55.153 --> 00:30:57.222
I will receive 6.25 bitcoins.

00:30:57.255 --> 00:30:58.690
It won't happen every time
 because there is

00:30:58.690 --> 00:31:00.825
a big competition of miners.

00:31:00.825 --> 00:31:03.127
6.25 bitcoin per block every ten minutes.

00:31:03.161 --> 00:31:06.130
That's quite a bit, especially if you
 think of the price of bitcoin today.

00:31:06.130 --> 00:31:10.902
Bitcoin is built in a way there is an entire
 incentive scheme that has been thought out

00:31:10.902 --> 00:31:15.940
by the creator of bitcoin that goes
 all the way to the year 2140 or so,

00:31:16.841 --> 00:31:19.210
which is that the amount of bitcoins

00:31:19.210 --> 00:31:22.380
that are produced will be divided
 every four years by two.

00:31:22.647 --> 00:31:26.217
So if I'm a miner today,
 I make 6.25 bitcoin per block

00:31:26.818 --> 00:31:30.088
that I confirm; four years from now
 after the next halving,

00:31:30.121 --> 00:31:34.225
it'll be half that - so, 3.12 bitcoin.

00:31:34.325 --> 00:31:40.098
This is an incentive for people
 to be as efficient as possible

00:31:40.098 --> 00:31:42.567
when running their bitcoin
 mining business,

00:31:43.301 --> 00:31:47.639
and also an incentive for the price
 to stay above a certain certain level.

00:31:47.872 --> 00:31:52.043
Because miners simply will either run
 out of business if the price is too low

00:31:53.344 --> 00:31:56.748
or will decide not to sell bitcoin
 because he's not covering

00:31:56.748 --> 00:31:59.450
for his operating expenses.

00:32:01.052 --> 00:32:04.555
In the bitcoin protocol,
 changes can be made in two ways.

00:32:05.089 --> 00:32:07.158
There's a simple way
 called a hard fork,

00:32:07.592 --> 00:32:09.827
where someone essentially
 makes a copy of bitcoin,

00:32:10.194 --> 00:32:13.631
makes changes to the protocol
 and releases it to the world.

00:32:14.232 --> 00:32:16.834
Miners need to connect
 to this new network and choose

00:32:16.834 --> 00:32:19.938
to use this new network
 over the original Bitcoin network.

00:32:20.805 --> 00:32:23.241
This was done by projects
 such as Bitcoin Cash

00:32:23.241 --> 00:32:24.909
and Bitcoin Satoshi Vision.

00:32:24.909 --> 00:32:26.878
They were trying to solve
 what the founders

00:32:26.878 --> 00:32:29.180
thought to be problems
 in the bitcoin network.

00:32:29.447 --> 00:32:31.182
But there was an a scaling attempt

00:32:31.182 --> 00:32:33.351
to allow bitcoin
 to continue to be money for the world.

00:32:33.685 --> 00:32:36.020
And it was called
 the SegWit2x agreement, where SegWit,

00:32:36.521 --> 00:32:38.890
which is if you're deep into crypto,
 you already know what that is,

00:32:38.890 --> 00:32:40.458
if not, don't worry about it.

00:32:40.458 --> 00:32:42.727
And the block size
 were going to be upgraded

00:32:42.727 --> 00:32:45.063
from one megabyte to two megabytes,
 which would,

00:32:45.063 --> 00:32:46.497
if you do both those things
 you're going to get more

00:32:46.497 --> 00:32:49.233
than double as many transactions
 available on the Bitcoin network.

00:32:49.467 --> 00:32:52.470
If you do just the 1 megabyte to
 2 megabyte, you double.

00:32:52.570 --> 00:32:55.340
At SegWit t's a little bit
 more on top of that anyhow.

00:32:55.740 --> 00:32:56.607
For whatever reason,

00:32:56.607 --> 00:32:58.977
the SegWit portion of that agreement
 was activated first.

00:32:59.010 --> 00:33:02.647
When that 2 megabyte upgrade
 eventually was aborted,

00:33:03.448 --> 00:33:06.417
I had to look around the world
 and say, OK, well, if I want a tool

00:33:06.417 --> 00:33:08.786
that can enable every human
 being on the planet to be able to send

00:33:08.786 --> 00:33:11.990
and receive any amount of money with
 any other human being on the planet,

00:33:12.590 --> 00:33:13.624
bitcoin can't do that.

00:33:13.624 --> 00:33:15.660
It's not going to do that
 with one megabyte blocks.

00:33:15.660 --> 00:33:17.061
It's impossible
 for bitcoin to do that.

00:33:17.061 --> 00:33:19.497
So there's a whole bunch
 of other cryptocurrencies other.

00:33:19.697 --> 00:33:21.532
Which one do I think
 is the most likely

00:33:21.532 --> 00:33:23.534
to bring the most economic freedom
 to the most people

00:33:23.534 --> 00:33:25.470
around the world
 in the shortest amount of time?

00:33:25.470 --> 00:33:27.305
And I looked at all
 the different cryptocurrencies out

00:33:27.305 --> 00:33:30.441
there, and Bitcoin Cash was the one
 that was at the top of my list.

00:33:31.075 --> 00:33:32.210
Since their launch,

00:33:32.210 --> 00:33:34.412
these projects have failed
 compared to bitcoin

00:33:34.712 --> 00:33:36.881
losing value
 against the first cryptocurrency

00:33:37.248 --> 00:33:40.518
because the people that are positioned
 in bitcoin didn't want to transition.

00:33:41.586 --> 00:33:45.990
This reluctance was mostly due
 to one reason - decentralization.

00:33:46.524 --> 00:33:50.862
Decentralization in bitcoin is the fact
 that no one controls the network.

00:33:51.629 --> 00:33:54.165
It is an open, secure network,

00:33:54.165 --> 00:33:56.601
controlled and improved
 by every participant.

00:33:57.235 --> 00:34:00.638
In projects like Bitcoin Cash,
 the number of participants

00:34:00.638 --> 00:34:04.208
being much lower than in bitcoin
 lowered the security.

00:34:04.709 --> 00:34:10.648
Bitcoin satoshi vision is a great example,
 as it was the victim of a 51% attack in 2021.

00:34:10.782 --> 00:34:12.917
A miners performance
 is based on the amount

00:34:12.917 --> 00:34:14.852
of computational power they have,

00:34:14.852 --> 00:34:18.256
and this is usually referred
 to as hash rate or hashing power.

00:34:18.856 --> 00:34:22.393
The mining power is distributed over
 different nodes across the world,

00:34:22.460 --> 00:34:25.263
which means it is
 not in the hands of a single entity.

00:34:25.596 --> 00:34:28.099
At least it is not supposed to be.

00:34:28.099 --> 00:34:31.469
But what happens when the hashrate
 is no longer distributed well enough?

00:34:31.869 --> 00:34:36.707
What happens if one single entity is able to
 obtain more than 50% of the hashing power?

00:34:37.308 --> 00:34:41.479
One possible consequence of
 that is what we call a 51% attack.

00:34:41.679 --> 00:34:44.315
Also known as a majority attack,

00:34:44.315 --> 00:34:48.186
a 51% attack is a potential attack
 on a blockchain network

00:34:48.186 --> 00:34:52.056
where a single entity or organization
 is able to control the majority

00:34:52.056 --> 00:34:55.226
of the hash rate, potentially
 causing a network disruption.

00:34:55.660 --> 00:34:58.896
In such a scenario, the attacker
 would have enough mining power

00:34:58.896 --> 00:35:02.533
to intentionally exclude or modify
 the ordering of transactions.

00:35:02.967 --> 00:35:06.537
They could also reverse transactions
 they made while being in control,

00:35:06.571 --> 00:35:08.439
leading to a double spending problem.

00:35:09.440 --> 00:35:12.477
A successful majority
 attack could also allow the attacker

00:35:12.477 --> 00:35:16.414
to prevent some or all transactions
 from being confirmed or to prevent

00:35:16.414 --> 00:35:19.951
miners from mining, resulting in
 what is known as mining monopoly.

00:35:20.451 --> 00:35:23.387
The second method to make changes
 is called a soft fork.

00:35:24.055 --> 00:35:27.992
In a soft fork, changes are made to
 the protocol and need to be accepted

00:35:27.992 --> 00:35:29.627
by the majority of miners.

00:35:30.061 --> 00:35:33.564
To do so, miners simply decide
 whether they want to upgrade

00:35:33.564 --> 00:35:35.666
to the new protocol
 and update their machines,

00:35:36.267 --> 00:35:38.936
and eventually,
 if approved by the majority of miners

00:35:39.337 --> 00:35:43.040
at a predetermined block number,
 the changes will be made official.

00:35:43.274 --> 00:35:46.310
And this is how miners
 will verify blocks from that point on.

00:35:47.879 --> 00:35:50.248
Unlike many other protocols,
 every change

00:35:50.248 --> 00:35:53.651
that is made to the bitcoin network
 needs to be backwards compatible.

00:35:54.285 --> 00:35:56.387
This means that
 if someone holds bitcoin,

00:35:56.387 --> 00:35:58.956
their bitcoin will still
 be valid after the changes.

00:35:59.991 --> 00:36:02.059
Other networks may be
 easier to change,

00:36:02.059 --> 00:36:06.130
but won't be backwards compatible
 and will often require holders of the coin

00:36:06.130 --> 00:36:09.200
to transfer their coins to platforms
 that will accept the changes

00:36:09.200 --> 00:36:12.303
before a certain date
 or risk losing everything.

00:36:13.638 --> 00:36:15.773
There will be a maximum

00:36:15.773 --> 00:36:19.010
and this is hardcoded,
 a maximum of 21 million bitcoin

00:36:19.277 --> 00:36:21.579
produced by the year 2140,

00:36:22.013 --> 00:36:24.682
and there will never be more of it.

00:36:24.682 --> 00:36:32.223
So we can't afford to lose some bitcoin
 simply based on a bug fix,

00:36:33.958 --> 00:36:36.827
functionality implementation,
 it's just something that cannot work.

00:36:37.094 --> 00:36:39.997
Therefore, every change
 has to be backwards compatible,

00:36:39.997 --> 00:36:42.700
and this makes it
 so much more difficult to change

00:36:43.100 --> 00:36:45.536
as opposed to other projects
 that don't care much

00:36:45.536 --> 00:36:48.940
because they have a management team
 that is there that will decide

00:36:49.140 --> 00:36:51.576
what new vision
 they have for this coin

00:36:51.943 --> 00:36:56.080
and will make updates regardless
 of what the community thinks,

00:36:56.080 --> 00:36:59.050
because they think it's what's
 in the best interest of the community.

00:37:00.051 --> 00:37:02.220
The fact bitcoin is so difficult to
 change

00:37:02.220 --> 00:37:05.189
is one of the reasons that make it
 a great asset for the long run.

00:37:05.856 --> 00:37:08.960
Buyers know what they're buying,
 and they know that the assets will

00:37:08.960 --> 00:37:10.461
remain the same.

00:37:10.494 --> 00:37:13.831
No other assets in the world
 provides this level of security.

00:37:14.432 --> 00:37:16.500
Other cryptocurrencies
 have been created,

00:37:16.701 --> 00:37:19.570
but were created by a group of people
 that still control

00:37:19.570 --> 00:37:22.573
the network and will make changes
 to profit themselves.

00:37:23.841 --> 00:37:26.877
Ethereum, for example,
 the second biggest cryptocurrency

00:37:26.877 --> 00:37:31.048
by market cap, is still controlled
 by the same team that created it.

00:37:31.749 --> 00:37:34.752
These people control the network
 and promote the network

00:37:34.752 --> 00:37:38.256
in order to bring in-experienced
 and non-technical investors

00:37:38.556 --> 00:37:41.659
that don't understand
 what a lack of decentralization means

00:37:41.993 --> 00:37:45.062
for the future of cryptocurrencies.

00:37:45.062 --> 00:37:48.432
The Ethereum network aims
 to change the way the protocol works.

00:37:48.866 --> 00:37:50.968
These changes
 will allow miners to mine

00:37:51.369 --> 00:37:54.205
only if they have logged
 32 ether beforehand.

00:37:55.273 --> 00:37:58.042
Anyone can mine bitcoin,
 but to mine etherium

00:37:58.342 --> 00:37:59.610
you will soon need to be part

00:37:59.610 --> 00:38:02.913
of the few in the world
 that can afford to buy 32 ether.

00:38:03.814 --> 00:38:07.451
This will make Ethereum just as good
 a currency as a fiat currency

00:38:07.918 --> 00:38:11.055
because the decisions of the few
 will impact the masses.

00:38:48.893 --> 00:38:53.030
Think of bitcoin, the asset
 and the network as the layer one,

00:38:53.264 --> 00:38:55.666
the other layers
 that are being built around it,

00:38:55.700 --> 00:38:58.135
they're called layer two's
 and also layer three's.

00:38:58.402 --> 00:39:02.973
But the the fastest growing layer
 to for bitcoin is called the Lightning Network

00:39:03.307 --> 00:39:06.777
and it's a different payment system

00:39:06.777 --> 00:39:10.247
that to use it, basically,
 I need to take some of my bitcoin and,

00:39:10.581 --> 00:39:14.352
you know, actually put it on
 to the Lightning Network

00:39:15.453 --> 00:39:17.088
and I need to open channels.

00:39:17.088 --> 00:39:18.589
If I open a tunnel
 with you, for example,

00:39:18.589 --> 00:39:20.491
it means I can transfer money to you.

00:39:20.491 --> 00:39:22.526
And the fact that I have
 this channel open with you

00:39:22.827 --> 00:39:25.896
means that I have access to the
 channels that you have open yourself.

00:39:27.031 --> 00:39:30.501
And it scales very quickly in the sense that
 if we both use the Lightning Network

00:39:30.501 --> 00:39:32.503
and we have a channel open
 between the two of us,

00:39:32.737 --> 00:39:35.373
then you have access to all of
 my contacts and you can pay them.

00:39:35.539 --> 00:39:37.508
And I have access to all of yours
 and can pay them.

00:39:37.508 --> 00:39:38.676
Payments that I do

00:39:38.676 --> 00:39:41.912
will go through the channels
 of maybe multiple people

00:39:42.713 --> 00:39:45.349
in order to reach its destination.

00:39:45.816 --> 00:39:49.487
And this allows for payments
 to actually be instant.

00:39:50.121 --> 00:39:51.522
Instant settlement.

00:39:51.522 --> 00:39:54.325
This doesn't exist in finance
 until now.

00:39:54.792 --> 00:39:57.795
And they're free
 or almost free in most cases

00:39:58.662 --> 00:40:02.900
just because of how the network
 is built and only once.

00:40:03.200 --> 00:40:06.237
I would bring this money
 out of the Lightning Network

00:40:06.237 --> 00:40:08.239
and back into the Bitcoin
 Main Network

00:40:08.572 --> 00:40:11.909
is a transaction actually done
 inside of this main network,

00:40:12.877 --> 00:40:14.912
which means that you can do

00:40:14.912 --> 00:40:18.048
a lot more transactions,
 much faster and cheaper,

00:40:19.183 --> 00:40:22.153
but still having bitcoin
 as the settlement layer

00:40:22.353 --> 00:40:24.688
that will confirm
 that all of this happened basically.

00:41:06.931 --> 00:41:13.170
The adverse environmental impacts
 of the computing activity used to mint

00:41:13.504 --> 00:41:16.841
many of these digital currencies
 in the first place.

00:41:17.641 --> 00:41:21.879
Bitcoin consumes
 more energy than entire countries

00:41:22.480 --> 00:41:26.717
and it is projected to consume
 as much energy

00:41:27.084 --> 00:41:32.790
as all the data centers
 in the whole world this year!

00:41:33.190 --> 00:41:35.893
One bitcoin transaction.

00:41:35.893 --> 00:41:39.196
A single purchase, sale or transfer

00:41:39.864 --> 00:41:43.501
uses the same amount of electricity
 as the typical U.S.

00:41:43.501 --> 00:41:46.470
household uses in more than a month.

00:41:46.604 --> 00:41:50.574
Bitcoin's main layer it's described
 as being very energy consuming,

00:41:51.175 --> 00:41:55.813
and it is a fact that at its highest
 in May 2021, the bitcoin network

00:41:55.813 --> 00:42:00.084
was using as much energy as what
 the entire country of Sweden uses.

00:42:01.685 --> 00:42:05.990
Although this seems like a lot,
we need to break down with this energy is

00:42:05.990 --> 00:42:08.959
where it comes from and
 how this trend is evolving,

00:42:08.959 --> 00:42:10.895
including where the big miners

00:42:10.895 --> 00:42:15.199
are transferring to in order to lower
 their cost and environmental impact.

00:42:15.900 --> 00:42:17.968
first of all,
 it is important to understand

00:42:17.968 --> 00:42:20.671
how a miner business is set up.

00:42:20.671 --> 00:42:22.740
These miners are businesses.

00:42:22.740 --> 00:42:24.208
The more important ones today,

00:42:24.208 --> 00:42:27.278
being international companies
 listed on stock exchanges,

00:42:28.112 --> 00:42:31.048
setting up a mining business requires
 the purchase of hardware.

00:42:31.582 --> 00:42:35.019
The computers needed to tap into
 the bitcoin network and start mining,

00:42:35.419 --> 00:42:38.055
as well as real estate to store
 all of the miners.

00:42:38.556 --> 00:42:43.260
Wherever miners are around the world,
 the prices for these two elements will often be similar.

00:42:43.861 --> 00:42:46.196
Of course,
 better deals can be negotiated.

00:42:46.497 --> 00:42:49.867
But given the price of electrical
 components and supply chain costs,

00:42:50.100 --> 00:42:53.337
this isn't where miners will often
 gain an edge over their competition.

00:42:53.871 --> 00:42:55.873
What matters most to miners is energy.

00:42:57.474 --> 00:43:01.845
The third factor in this is the most important
 one is the energy

00:43:01.845 --> 00:43:05.316
that they spend in order
 to make their mining rigs turn.

00:43:05.449 --> 00:43:07.251
The prices vary between countries,

00:43:07.251 --> 00:43:11.956
they vary between the region and they vary
 on the source of energy that they choose.

00:43:11.956 --> 00:43:18.562
The cheapest source is right now, always renewable
 energies or nuclear, but nuclear is a bit

00:43:18.762 --> 00:43:20.831
killed by governments.

00:43:20.831 --> 00:43:26.236
So these companies, by default,
 they have to direct themselves

00:43:26.503 --> 00:43:30.774
and push for the development
 of these more renewable

00:43:31.308 --> 00:43:33.978
energies because it will cost
 them cheaper in the long term.

00:43:34.278 --> 00:43:36.680
Cheap energy can be found
 from two sources;

00:43:37.414 --> 00:43:40.117
green energy and wasted energy.

00:43:41.485 --> 00:43:44.021
The energy we produce is
 always in excess.

00:43:44.355 --> 00:43:48.192
We never consume 100% of the energy
 produced by power plants

00:43:48.759 --> 00:43:51.762
because it would put communities
 at risk of having blackouts.

00:43:52.596 --> 00:43:59.203
In the US, it is estimated that 5-6% of the
 energy produced is lost when it is in transit,

00:43:59.770 --> 00:44:02.539
which is around 211 terawatt hours.

00:44:03.507 --> 00:44:05.709
This amounts alone is close to double

00:44:05.709 --> 00:44:08.946
what Bitcoin was consuming at its
 all time high.

00:44:11.849 --> 00:44:16.420
Nowadays, governments are pushing
 for the adoption of solar and wind power

00:44:16.420 --> 00:44:18.522
and decide to crack down on nuclear.

00:44:19.223 --> 00:44:25.963
Unfortunately, although on paper this sounds
 positive, there are adverse effects to this.

00:44:25.963 --> 00:44:29.600
With our current technologies,
 the energy produced cannot be stocked.

00:44:30.234 --> 00:44:32.736
Therefore, the energy from solar
 and wind

00:44:33.070 --> 00:44:35.606
can only be produced
 when it is sunny or windy.

00:44:36.507 --> 00:44:40.310
This makes it very unreliable
 and requires backup power plants to be

00:44:40.310 --> 00:44:44.615
present and active on a daily basis
 to cover for this lack of energy.

00:44:45.683 --> 00:44:49.019
This really is an adverse effect
 because Germany, for example,

00:44:49.420 --> 00:44:51.989
the biggest adopter of solar
 and wind in Europe,

00:44:52.556 --> 00:44:56.160
is now back to producing
 the same amount of CO2 levels

00:44:56.493 --> 00:44:58.429
as it was producing 20 years ago.

00:45:00.130 --> 00:45:03.100
Green policies around the world
 have stopped the adoption

00:45:03.100 --> 00:45:06.637
of nuclear power plants
 and have indirectly forced the world

00:45:06.970 --> 00:45:09.473
to consume more natural gas
 than it used to.

00:45:10.741 --> 00:45:13.911
Natural gas comes
 with its own set of problems,

00:45:14.878 --> 00:45:16.880
just like other energy sources.

00:45:16.880 --> 00:45:21.385
Natural gas is produced more than that
 is consumed, which leads to flaring.

00:45:21.385 --> 00:45:26.924
Flaring  is simply the excess gas that
 is extracted that needs to be burned.

00:45:27.791 --> 00:45:31.095
It is a standard and a necessary habit
 in the industry.

00:45:31.862 --> 00:45:36.166
In 2019 alone,
 it is estimated that 150 billion

00:45:36.166 --> 00:45:39.436
cubic meters of natural gas
 was flared in the world.

00:45:40.471 --> 00:45:44.475
This is the same amount as Japan
 and Korea imported that same year,

00:45:45.542 --> 00:45:49.913
all gone, in the air, producing
 approximately 300 million

00:45:49.913 --> 00:45:54.551
tons of CO2, the same as the total
 annual emissions of Italy.

00:45:57.354 --> 00:46:01.325
In order  to lower their cost and energy,
 miners are directly connecting

00:46:01.325 --> 00:46:05.562
to these sources of energy
 that until now were inaccessible.

00:46:06.597 --> 00:46:09.500
Because bitcoin miners
 can be placed anywhere in the world

00:46:09.700 --> 00:46:11.802
without the need
 of being close to communities,

